================================================================================
                   EX03 — At least this beats coffee-making
================================================================================

WHAT THIS EXERCISE COVERS:
  - Everything from ex02 (AForm, Bureaucrat, all 3 concrete forms)
  - A NEW class: Intern
  - Intern has NO name, NO grade, NO unique characteristics
  - Intern has ONE method: makeForm(std::string formName, std::string target)
  - makeForm() returns a pointer to a new Form (AForm*) based on formName
  - Factory pattern: creating objects based on a string identifier
  - Avoiding long if/else chains (use function pointers or a lookup table)

HOW makeForm() WORKS:
  - Takes two strings: the form name and the target
  - Returns a NEW form object allocated with new
  - Prints: "Intern creates <form name>"
  - If the form name doesn't exist: print an error message
  - Valid form names:
      "shrubbery creation"    → new ShrubberyCreationForm(target)
      "robotomy request"      → new RobotomyRequestForm(target)
      "presidential pardon"   → new PresidentialPardonForm(target)

================================================================================
                         FILES TO CREATE
================================================================================

[FILE 1] Intern.hpp
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - Class Intern with OCF (default ctor, copy ctor, operator=, destructor)
  - Method: AForm* makeForm(std::string const & formName,
                             std::string const & target);
  - Include AForm.hpp (or forward declare AForm)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------

[FILE 2] Intern.cpp
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - OCF implementations (can be mostly empty — Intern has no data)
  - makeForm() implementation:
      Option A (clean — function pointer array):
          Define an array of { "form name", createFunction } pairs.
          Loop through the array, if formName matches → call createFunction.
          If no match → print error.

      Option B (simple — if/else):
          if (formName == "shrubbery creation") return new ShrubberyCreationForm(target);
          else if (formName == "robotomy request") return new RobotomyRequestForm(target);
          else if (formName == "presidential pardon") return new PresidentialPardonForm(target);
          else { print error; return NULL; }

      Option A is cleaner and avoids the "ugly if/else" the subject warns about.

