      - Intern creates "shrubbery creation" form → verify it works
      - Intern creates "robotomy request" form → sign and execute it
      - Intern creates "presidential pardon" form → sign and execute it
      - Intern creates "nonexistent form" → should print error, return NULL
      - Don't forget to delete the AForm* pointers (no leaks!)
      - EACH test in its OWN try/catch

================================================================================
                            TRAPS / PITFALLS
================================================================================

  TRAP 1: Memory leaks — makeForm returns new'd memory
    The CALLER is responsible for deleting the returned pointer.
    AForm* f = intern.makeForm("robotomy request", "Bender");
    ... use f ...
    delete f;  ← DON'T FORGET

  TRAP 2: Intern has no data — OCF is trivial
    Default constructor, copy constructor, operator=, destructor can all
    be mostly empty. Intern has no attributes to copy/assign.
    Still MUST exist (subject requires OCF).

  TRAP 3: Return NULL for unknown form names, not throw
    The subject says to print an error message. It does NOT say to throw.
    Return NULL and let the caller check.

  TRAP 4: Case sensitivity in form names
    The subject examples use lowercase: "shrubbery creation", etc.
    Match exactly what the subject asks — don't add case-insensitive matching
    unless asked.

  TRAP 5: Don't use a giant if/else or switch
    The subject explicitly warns against "ugly" approaches.
    Use an array of pairs (string + function pointer) and loop through it.
    This is cleaner and more extensible.

  TRAP 6: Virtual destructor matters here
    When you do: delete f; where f is AForm*, the virtual destructor
    ensures the correct derived destructor is called.
    If you didn't fix this in ex02, it'll leak in ex03.

================================================================================
                       EXAMPLE CLEAN makeForm() PATTERN
================================================================================

  // In Intern.cpp — function pointer approach (C++98 compatible)

  typedef AForm* (*FormCreator)(std::string const & target);

  static AForm* createShrubbery(std::string const & target) {
      return new ShrubberyCreationForm(target);
  }
  static AForm* createRobotomy(std::string const & target) {
      return new RobotomyRequestForm(target);
  }
  static AForm* createPresidential(std::string const & target) {
      return new PresidentialPardonForm(target);
  }

  AForm* Intern::makeForm(std::string const & formName,
                           std::string const & target) {
      std::string names[] = {
          "shrubbery creation",
          "robotomy request",
          "presidential pardon"
      };
      FormCreator creators[] = {
          &createShrubbery,
          &createRobotomy,
          &createPresidential
      };

      for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
          if (formName == names[i]) {
              std::cout << "Intern creates " << formName << std::endl;
              return creators[i](target);
          }
      }
      std::cout << "Intern can't create \"" << formName
                << "\": unknown form name" << std::endl;
      return NULL;
  }
